{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgZANzimURUv9Mr8isDre","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QALuVGN6YmD5EYH3kYaN5Q","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/temperature_calibration_gaps_20261030_120000_ab12cd34; cat > REPORT.md <<'EOF'\n# Temperature Calibration Gaps: When Softmax Temperature Fails to Control Output Diversity\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\n**Question.** How do output diversity and quality change with softmax temperature (T = 0–2.0) on open-ended, factual\nand coding tasks, and does the useful range depend on model size and family?\n\n**Main finding.** Temperature does not fail gradually. Quality stays almost flat up to a model-specific point and then\ndrops sharply. That point depends strongly on the model: story coherence falls below 90% of peak at T ≈ 0.84 for\nQwen2.5-0.5B and T ≈ 1.31 for Qwen2.5-14B. The hypothesis's fixed thresholds (0.7, 0.8, 1.2) describe no single model\nwell, and the coding clause is largely wrong for models of 3B and up.\n\n| Clause | Verdict | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Open-ended: diversity plateaus above 1.2, coherence drops 20–40% | **Partly supported** | Measured diversity stops rising in 9/10 models, but it peaks at T = 1.0–1.4 and then falls. Coherence loss is usually larger than predicted: 48–63% at T = 1.4 in 9/10 models |\n| Factual QA: above 0.7 accuracy degrades with no meaningful diversity gain | **Mostly supported, small effect** | T = 0.8–1.0 is worse than T ≤ 0.7 in 9/10 models, but only by 0.7–5.2 points. Coverage over 4 samples gains nothing further above 0.7 |\n| Code: narrow useful range 0.2–0.6, sharp collapse above 0.8 | **Refuted as stated** | No model drops significantly from 0.6 to 0.8. Greedy (T = 0) is best or tied-best for 5/10 models, and models ≥3B in the Qwen family and Gemma stay within 90% of peak to T ≈ 1.1–1.4 |\n| Smaller models have narrower useful ranges | **Supported for Qwen, weak for Llama** | Qwen ladder: yes on all three tasks. Llama ladder: QA only; story cliff does not move from 1B to 8B |\n| Curve shape differs across families at the same scale | **Supported** | Llama differs from the other three families by 4–8× the seed noise floor; Qwen and Mistral are close to each other |\n\n**Practical implication.** Below T ≈ 0.8 temperature is safe on nearly every model tested, but it is a weak diversity\ncontrol. Above T ≈ 1.0, check the specific model: identical settings can give intact output on one model and word\nsalad on another of the same size.\n\nNo API key was available, so all subject models are open-weight checkpoints run locally. GPT, Claude and Gemini were\nnot tested and these results should not be assumed to apply to them.\n\n## 2. Research Question & Hypothesis\n\nTestable sub-hypotheses (H1–H5) are defined in `planning.md`:\n\n- **H1 open-ended:** diversity gain is much smaller above T = 1.2, while coherence at T ≥ 1.4 is 20–40% below peak.\n- **H2 factual QA:** accuracy above 0.7 falls, with little gain in coverage.\n- **H3 code:** useful range ≈ 0.2–0.6, sharp collapse above 0.8.\n- **H4 size:** the upper end of the useful range grows with model size.\n- **H5 family:** curves differ across families at matched size.\n\n## 3. Literature Context\n\nThe resource-finding stage produced no `literature_review.md`, `papers/` or `code/`. The works below are cited **from\nmemory and were not re-checked against the papers**, so treat their details as unverified:\n\n- Holtzman et al. (2020): degeneration at high randomness motivated nucleus sampling.\n- Chen et al. (2021, Codex): best pass@1 at low T, best pass@k at moderate T. Our pass@4 pattern is consistent with this.\n- Renze & Guven (2024): accuracy broadly flat over T in [0, 1].\n- Peeperkorn et al. (2024): temperature only weakly raises story novelty and costs coherence.\n\nThe intended contribution is one controlled grid across tasks, sizes and families, extending to T = 2.0.\n\n## 4. Methodology\n\n### Models\nTen models, plus two controls. All use pure temperature sampling (`top_p=1`, `top_k` off, `min_p=0`,\n`repetition_penalty=1`, `generation_config=\"vllm\"`), with vLLM 0.32 on NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPUs.\n\n| Key | Checkpoint | Params | Precision |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| qwen2.5-0.5b / -1.5b | Qwen/Qwen2.5-{0.5,1.5}B-Instruct | 0.5B / 1.5B | bf16 |\n| qwen2.5-3b / -7b / -14b | Qwen/Qwen2.5-{3,7,14}B-Instruct-AWQ | 3.1B / 7.6B / 14.8B | AWQ-INT4 |\n| llama3.2-1b | unsloth/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct | 1.2B | bf16 |\n| llama3.2-3b | AMead10/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-AWQ | 3.2B | AWQ-INT4 |\n| llama3.1-8b | hugging-quants/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-AWQ-INT4 | 8.0B | AWQ-INT4 |\n| mistral-7b | solidrust/Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3-AWQ | 7.2B | AWQ-INT4 |\n| gemma2-9b | hugging-quants/gemma-2-9b-it-AWQ-INT4 | 9.2B | AWQ-INT4 |\n| *control:* qwen2.5-0.5b-seed2 | same as 0.5B, different sampling seeds | 0.5B | bf16 |\n| *control:* qwen2.5-7b-bf16 | Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct | 7.6B | bf16 |\n\n### Design\n- **Temperatures:** 0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 1.0, 1.2, 1.4, 1.6, 2.0.\n- **Samples:** 4 per (prompt, T); 1 at T = 0, which is deterministic. That is 23,124 samples per run and about 277,000\n  in total across 12 runs. All 12 runs completed all 6,204 (prompt, T) cells.\n- **Stories:** 100 WritingPrompts test prompts, up to 256 tokens.\n- **Factual QA:** 300 closed-book TriviaQA validation questions, up to 24 tokens.\n- **Code:** all 164 HumanEval problems, up to 448 tokens, executed against the official tests in a resource-limited\n  subprocess. Full prompts are in `src/prep_data.py`.\n\n### Metrics\n- **Stories, diversity:** mean pairwise cosine distance of `all-mpnet-base-v2` embeddings (`sem_div`), and distinct-2\n  over pooled samples.\n- **Stories, quality:** Qwen3-8B judge, 1–9, probability-weighted digit, on 2 of 4 samples (24,200 judgments across\n  the 12 runs at 2,100 per run... see note); \"coherent rate\" = share scoring ≥5; lexical validity.\n- **QA:** per-sample accuracy (normalized alias match), coverage@4 (any of 4 correct), distinct answers.\n- **Code:** pass@1, pass@4, syntax validity, distinct programs by AST.\n- **Useful range:** temperatures with quality ≥ 90% of the model's peak, linearly interpolated; T_u is the upper\n  bound. Story quality here is the coherent rate.\n\nAll 25,200 judgments (2,100 per run) passed the logprob validity checks, with none falling back to text parsing.\n\n### Statistics\nItem-level bootstrap (2,000 resamples) for CIs, paired sign-flip permutation tests with Holm correction, and Spearman\ncorrelation for size trends. Curve-shape gaps are compared with two measured floors: a seed replicate and a\nbf16-vs-AWQ control.\n\n### Changes from the plan\n- **14B and 3B models moved to AWQ.** Shared-GPU memory ruled out 14B bf16, and 3B moved to fit a small idle slice.\n  Precision therefore co-varies with size in both ladders (bf16 ≤1.5B, AWQ ≥3B). Section 5.6 shows the effect on curve\n  shape is small but the effect on level is not zero.\n- **Story useful range uses the coherent rate, not the raw judge score.** This was decided after seeing Qwen2.5-0.5B\n  and Llama-1B results, because the judge rarely scores below 3.\n- **The judge rubric was edited once** after inspecting five samples, before any scored judging run.\n- **A seed replicate was added** as a noise floor, and Holm correction and ladder contrasts were added to the analysis.\n  Several analysis choices were therefore made after seeing partial data.\n\n## 5. Results\n\n![All curves](figures/curves_all.png)\n\n### 5.1 H1: open-ended generation\n![Story frontier](figures/story_frontier.png)\n\n| Model | Peak `sem_div` at T | Slope ratio, `sem_div` (1.2–2.0 / 0.2–1.2) | Slope ratio, distinct-2 | Judge drop at 1.2 | at 1.4 | at 2.0 | Coherent rate at 1.2 | at 1.4 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| qwen2.5-0.5b | 1.2 | −1.00 | 0.03 | 44% | 50% | 50% | 0.03 | 0.01 |\n| qwen2.5-1.5b | 1.2 | −0.38 | 0.07 | 53% | 60% | 61% | 0.07 | 0.00 |\n| qwen2.5-3b | 1.4 | −0.45 | 0.23 | 17% | 48% | 62% | 0.86 | 0.22 |\n| qwen2.5-7b | 1.4 | −0.47 | 0.38 | 17% | 53% | 64% | 0.88 | 0.20 |\n| qwen2.5-14b | 1.6 | **+0.98** | 0.43 | 3% | 19% | 63% | 1.00 | 0.82 |\n| llama3.2-1b | 1.0 | −2.09 | 0.05 | 58% | 61% | 62% | 0.04 | 0.00 |\n| llama3.2-3b | 1.0 | −1.41 | 0.12 | 55% | 62% | 62% | 0.10 | 0.00 |\n| llama3.1-8b | 1.0 | −1.65 | 0.09 | 57% | 63% | 63% | 0.07 | 0.00 |\n| mistral-7b | 1.4 | −0.81 | 0.42 | 15% | 49% | 63% | 1.00 | 0.13 |\n| gemma2-9b | 1.4 | −1.02 | 0.26 | 10% | 53% | 64% | 0.95 | 0.09 |\n\n- **Plateau:** supported in the sense that diversity stops improving, but \"plateau\" is the wrong shape. Semantic\n  diversity rises, peaks and falls in 9/10 models. Qwen2.5-14B is the exception, with no slowdown between the two\n  ranges (ratio 0.98, 95% CI 0.69–1.30).\n- **Most of the \"plateau\" is measurement.** Distinct-2 flattens because it reaches its ceiling of 1.0. The fall in\n  `sem_div` arises because word salad from different prompts embeds similarly, so it does not mean the outputs are\n  less varied.\n- **Diversity among coherent outputs keeps rising.** Restricted to prompts where both judged samples are coherent,\n  `sem_div` increases at every T until too few coherent groups remain\n  (`results/analysis/sem_div_coherent_groups.csv`). This is a selected subset, but it suggests coherence loss, not\n  saturation, is what ends useful diversity.\n- **Coherence:** the 20–40% prediction is met only in passing. At T = 1.4 one model falls in or near that range\n  (14B, 19%); nine lose 48–63%. Because the judge's floor is about 3, these figures understate the damage, and the\n  raw scale cannot fall more than about 64%. The coherent rate shows it better: it goes from about 1.0 to about 0 within\n  one or two grid steps.\n\n### 5.2 H2: factual QA\n\n| Model | Greedy acc | Mean T ≤ 0.7 | Mean T 0.8–1.0 | Difference | Holm p | Mean T ≥ 1.2 | Coverage@4, T = 0.7 | Coverage@4, T = 1.0 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| qwen2.5-0.5b | .140 | .134 | .090 | −4.4 pt | <.001 | .030 | .217 | .167 |\n| qwen2.5-1.5b | .370 | .360 | .330 | −2.9 pt | .001 | .198 | .467 | .430 |\n| qwen2.5-3b | .410 | .393 | .367 | −2.6 pt | .001 | .234 | .447 | .463 |\n| qwen2.5-7b | .553 | .545 | .522 | −2.2 pt | .001 | .348 | .593 | .603 |\n| qwen2.5-14b | .627 | .621 | .615 | −0.7 pt | .217 | .512 | .653 | .660 |\n| llama3.2-1b | .317 | .314 | .262 | −5.2 pt | <.001 | .104 | .410 | .383 |\n| llama3.2-3b | .553 | .545 | .497 | −4.8 pt | <.001 | .246 | .613 | .623 |\n| llama3.1-8b | .700 | .692 | .642 | −5.0 pt | <.001 | .346 | .763 | .760 |\n| mistral-7b | .723 | .713 | .693 | −2.0 pt | <.001 | .548 | .753 | .757 |\n| gemma2-9b | .753 | .744 | .719 | −2.5 pt | .001 | .435 | .810 | .800 |\n\nThe p-values above are from 10,000 sign-flip permutations, so \"<.001\" is the resolution limit.\n\nAccuracy falls significantly in 9/10 models, but the loss is small. The degradation is gradual up to about T = 1.2 and\nsteep only beyond it. Sampling does help somewhat at or below 0.7: coverage@4 is 2.7–9.7 points above greedy\naccuracy at T = 0.7. Moving from 0.7 to 1.0 adds nothing on average (changes range from −5.0 to +1.6 points), even\nthough distinct answers keep rising. The extra diversity above 0.7 is mostly more wrong answers.\n\n### 5.3 H3: code generation\n\n| Model | Greedy pass@1 | Best T | T_u (95% CI) | T50 | pass@1 at 0.6 | 0.8 | 1.0 | 1.2 | Holm p, 0.6 vs 1.0 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| qwen2.5-0.5b | .360 | 0.4 | 0.58 (0.41–0.64) | 1.09 | .323 | .273 | .232 | .123 | <.001 |\n| qwen2.5-1.5b | .585 | 0 | 0.81 (0.34–0.87) | 1.29 | .535 | .535 | .415 | .354 | <.001 |\n| qwen2.5-3b | .640 | 0.2 | 1.07 (0.81–1.22) | 1.70 | .630 | .642 | .601 | .570 | .916 |\n| qwen2.5-7b | .768 | 0.7 | 1.42 (1.07–1.46) | 1.79 | .770 | .759 | .739 | .710 | .392 |\n| qwen2.5-14b | .841 | 0 | 1.15 (0.56–1.43) | 1.82 | .787 | .793 | .780 | .750 | .946 |\n| llama3.2-1b | .341 | 0 | 0.44 (0.16–0.68) | 1.17 | .276 | .284 | .230 | .160 | .038 |\n| llama3.2-3b | .549 | 0 | 0.72 (0.31–0.88) | 1.23 | .497 | .486 | .398 | .312 | <.001 |\n| llama3.1-8b | .628 | 0 | 0.69 (0.37–0.91) | 1.26 | .582 | .537 | .532 | .381 | .085 |\n| mistral-7b | .268 | 0.7 | 0.78 (0.14–1.05) | 1.39 | .264 | .238 | .241 | .191 | .946 |\n| gemma2-9b | .610 | 0.4 | 1.34 (0.60–1.41) | 1.65 | .591 | .616 | .607 | .595 | .946 |\n\n- **No sharp collapse just above 0.8.** No model has a significant drop from 0.6 to 0.8 after Holm correction.\n  From 0.6 to 1.0, four models drop significantly, and all are ≤3B.\n- **The lower bound of 0.2 is not supported.** T_lo = 0 for every model. Temperature gives no reliable pass@1 benefit,\n  and five models peak at greedy. T_u confidence intervals are wide because the curves are flat near the threshold.\n- **Pass@4 is where temperature pays off.** At T = 0.8 pass@4 exceeds greedy by up to 16.5 points (Qwen2.5-1.5B),\n  and by 6–15 points for the other models. If several attempts can be tested, moderate T helps.\n- **The real collapse is later and is syntactic.** Pooled across models, syntax errors go from 2% at T = 0.8 to 17% at\n  1.2, 45% at 1.4 and 93% at 2.0.\n\n### 5.4 H4: model size\n![Useful ranges](figures/useful_ranges.png)\n\n| Task | Qwen 0.5B → 14B ΔT_u (95% CI) | Llama 1B → 8B ΔT_u (95% CI) | Spearman ρ, all 10 models (p) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Stories | +0.47 (0.43, 0.55) | −0.05 (−0.09, 0.00) | 0.61 (.060) |\n| QA | +0.87 (0.76, 1.11) | +0.27 (0.04, 0.48) | 0.90 (<.001) |\n| Code | +0.57 (0.00, 0.93) | +0.25 (−0.15, 0.59) | 0.64 (.048) |\n\nLarger models tolerate higher temperature in the Qwen family on all three tasks, though code only just clears zero.\nIn the Llama family it holds for QA only. Story T_u is 1.01 at 1B, 1.00 at 3B and 0.96 at 8B. Spearman values within a\nladder are based on 3–5 points and are not meaningful alone, which is why the bootstrap contrasts are shown.\n\nCode T50, which is less sensitive than T_u, also rises with size: 1.09 → 1.82 for Qwen and 1.17 → 1.26 for Llama.\n\n### 5.5 H5: family differences at matched scale\n![Family comparison](figures/family_matched_diversity.png)\n\nShape gap = mean absolute gap between curves normalized to their own maxima. Floors for `sem_div`: seed 0.031,\nprecision 0.026.\n\n| Pair (7–9B, all AWQ) | `sem_div` shape gap | Ts with CI excluding 0 (of 11) | Peak T |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Qwen 7B vs Llama 8B | 0.220 | 9 | 1.4 vs 1.0 |\n| Llama 8B vs Mistral 7B | 0.205 | 10 | 1.0 vs 1.4 |\n| Llama 8B vs Gemma 9B | 0.118 | 8 | 1.0 vs 1.4 |\n| Qwen 7B vs Gemma 9B | 0.104 | 8 | 1.4 vs 1.4 |\n| Mistral 7B vs Gemma 9B | 0.098 | 9 | 1.4 vs 1.4 |\n| Qwen 7B vs Mistral 7B | 0.046 | 2 | 1.4 vs 1.4 |\n\nThe 1–1.5B and 3B Qwen-vs-Llama pairs show the same result (gaps 0.225 and 0.224). In stories, family matters more\nthan size in this sample: Llama-8B breaks at T_u = 0.96, close to Llama-1B at 1.01, while the other 7–9B models hold\nto 1.17–1.22. The study cannot explain why. Logit scale and vocabulary are candidates, but neither was measured.\n\nEach family is represented by one checkpoint per size, so \"family\" here cannot be separated from individual\ncheckpoint or quantizer differences.\n\n### 5.6 Controls\n- **Seed:** two sampling-seed runs of Qwen2.5-0.5B differ at no temperature for any metric.\n- **Precision:** comparing Qwen2.5-7B bf16 with AWQ, curve shapes are at the seed floor (`sem_div` gap 0.026). Levels\n  do differ: greedy pass@1 is .829 vs .768, and greedy QA .573 vs .553. AWQ therefore does not explain the shape\n  differences, but absolute levels should not be compared across precisions.\n\n### 5.7 Failure modes (pooled over the 10 main models)\n\n| T | Code passes | Syntax errors | Truncated code | QA correct | QA rambling >8 words | Stories truncated | Story non-ASCII chars |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 0.7 | 52% | 2% | 2% | 49% | 3% | 10% | 0.1% |\n| 1.0 | 48% | 4% | 2% | 47% | 2% | 14% | 0.5% |\n| 1.2 | 42% | 17% | 9% | 43% | 4% | 47% | 1.7% |\n| 1.4 | 32% | 45% | 35% | 37% | 12% | 70% | 5.5% |\n| 1.6 | 21% | 68% | 61% | 29% | 28% | 87% | 9.0% |\n| 2.0 | 5% | 93% | 89% | 11% | 58% | 92% | 11.4% |\n\nAbove the cliff, models stop terminating, drift into other languages and emit code tokens in prose. A random high-T\ntoken that breaks the text seems to make further random tokens more likely, which would fit the abrupt shape, though\nthis mechanism was not tested directly.\n\n## 6. Limitations\n\n- **One judge, compressed scale.** The Qwen3-8B judge rarely scores below 3, and it shares a lineage with the Qwen\n  subjects. A manual audit of 16 boundary stories agreed with the ≥5 threshold on 12 (75%), with the judge slightly\n  conservative. That audit was by the researcher-agent, on one model, and is small (`results/judge_audit.md`).\n- **The embedding metric is misleading on degenerate text,** as described in 5.1.\n- **Family and checkpoint are confounded,** and precision co-varies with size.\n- **Mistral's low code score** (.268) is below what its QA score would suggest. Restoring prompt imports raised it\n  only to .280, and failures are mostly real assertion errors, but the cause is unexplained. It may be specific to\n  this checkpoint or prompt, so its code level should not be taken as representative of Mistral-7B.\n- **Scope.** Pure temperature only, with no top-p or top-k. Tests cover short stories, one prompt per task, and 4\n  samples per cell. T = 0 uses one sample, and a single seed replicate is a thin noise floor. vLLM batch composition can\n  also change outputs slightly.\n- **Grid resolution.** Steps of 0.2 above T = 0.8 limit how precisely a cliff can be placed, and T_u depends on the\n  90% threshold choice. Bootstrap CIs on story T_u are narrow partly because of this interpolation.\n- **Multiple comparisons** were corrected within tests but not across the full set of hypotheses.\n- **Contamination:** TriviaQA, HumanEval and WritingPrompts are public and may be in training data. This matters\n  little for curve shapes, more for absolute levels.\n\n## 7. Conclusions & Next Steps\n\nTemperature's useful range is set by where each model breaks, and that varies widely. There is no universal threshold,\nand the common 0.7 default is safe rather than optimal. Single-attempt code and QA do best at low T, while pass@4 and\ncoverage@4 benefit from moderate T.\n\nSuggested follow-ups:\n\n1. Measure logit scale and the top-token margin per model to test whether they predict the cliff location.\n2. Add more checkpoints per family to separate family from checkpoint.\n3. Run hosted frontier models through the same grid when an API key is available.\n4. Replace `sem_div` with a measure that does not collapse on degenerate text, and add human coherence ratings.\n5. Sample T at 0.05 steps around each model's cliff to test how abrupt it really is.\n\n## 8. Reproducibility\n\n- **Environment:** Python 3.12, vLLM 0.32.0, torch 2.13.0, transformers 5.19.0 (`results/environment.txt`, `uv.lock`).\n  Seeds are set in `src/worker.py` and `src/analyze.py`.\n- **Hardware:** RTX A6000 (48 GB), shared; ~3 h 15 min wall time. Runs used 4–22 GB slices and several parallel\n  workers, so timing does not generalize. No API cost.\n- **Run:** `./run_all.sh`. It needs an OpenAI-compatible Qwen3-8B server on port 8011.\n- **Outputs:** raw generations in `results/gens/`, scores in `results/scores/`, judgments in `results/judge/`, tables\n  in `results/analysis/`.\n\n## References\n\nCited from memory; not verified in this session.\n\n- Chen, M. et al. (2021). Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code.\n- Holtzman, A. et al. (2020). The Curious Case of Neural Text Degeneration. ICLR.\n- Peeperkorn, M. et al. (2024). Is Temperature the Creativity Parameter of Large Language Models?\n- Renze, M. & Guven, E. (2024). The Effect of Sampling Temperature on Problem Solving in Large Language Models.\n\nDatasets: `euclaise/writingprompts`, `mandarjoshi/trivia_qa`, `openai/openai_humaneval`.\nEOF\npython3 - <<'EOF'\np='REPORT.md'; s=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"on 2 of 4 samples (24,200 judgments across\\n  the 12 runs at 2,100 per run... see note); \\\"coherent rate\\\"\",\"on 2 of 4 samples; \\\"coherent rate\\\"\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"see note\" REPORT.md; .venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport os; os.environ.setdefault('NUMPY_MADVISE_HUGEPAGE','0')\nimport pandas as pd\nc=pd.read_csv('/tmp/h3_run1.csv').set_index('model')\nbase=pd.read_csv('results/analysis/curves.csv') if False else None\nh=pd.read_csv('/tmp/curves_run1.csv')\np=h[(h.task=='code')&(h.metric=='passk')].pivot_table(index='model',columns='T',values='mean')\nprint((p[0.8]-p[0.0]).round(3).to_string())\nq=h[(h.task=='qa')&(h.metric=='cover')].pivot_table(index='model',columns='T',values='mean'); print((q[1.0]-q[0.7]).round(3).to_string())\nprint(c.sort_values('p_0.6_vs_0.8_holm')[['p_0.6_vs_0.8_holm','p_0.6_vs_1.0_holm']].round(3).to_string())\nEOF","description":"Write REPORT.md and check quoted figures","timeout":60000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4867,"cache_read_input_tokens":279503,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4867},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2332c03a-08c0-4a8b-a3ed-e94c26a471d1","uuid":"08c17098-7ef6-4d5a-aed6-3108ef8cb068","timestamp":"2026-10-30T21:53:20.521Z","request_id":"req_011CgZANzKTHPBf4pvV65yab","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QALuVGN6YmD5EYH3kYaN5Q":{"command":"cd /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/temperature_calibration_gaps_20261030_120000_ab12cd34; cat > REPORT.md <<'EOF'\n# Temperature Calibration Gaps: When Softmax Temperature Fails to Control Output Diversity\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\n**Question.** How do output diversity and quality change with softmax temperature (T = 0–2.0) on open-ended, factual\nand coding tasks, and does the useful range depend on model size and family?\n\n**Main finding.** Temperature does not fail gradually. Quality stays almost flat up to a model-specific point and then\ndrops sharply. That point depends strongly on the model: story coherence falls below 90% of peak at T ≈ 0.84 for\nQwen2.5-0.5B and T ≈ 1.31 for Qwen2.5-14B. The hypothesis's fixed thresholds (0.7, 0.8, 1.2) describe no single model\nwell, and the coding clause is largely wrong for models of 3B and up.\n\n| Clause | Verdict | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Open-ended: diversity plateaus above 1.2, coherence drops 20–40% | **Partly supported** | Measured diversity stops rising in 9/10 models, but it peaks at T = 1.0–1.4 and then falls. Coherence loss is usually larger than predicted: 48–63% at T = 1.4 in 9/10 models |\n| Factual QA: above 0.7 accuracy degrades with no meaningful diversity gain | **Mostly supported, small effect** | T = 0.8–1.0 is worse than T ≤ 0.7 in 9/10 models, but only by 0.7–5.2 points. Coverage over 4 samples gains nothing further above 0.7 |\n| Code: narrow useful range 0.2–0.6, sharp collapse above 0.8 | **Refuted as stated** | No model drops significantly from 0.6 to 0.8. Greedy (T = 0) is best or tied-best for 5/10 models, and models ≥3B in the Qwen family and Gemma stay within 90% of peak to T ≈ 1.1–1.4 |\n| Smaller models have narrower useful ranges | **Supported for Qwen, weak for Llama** | Qwen ladder: yes on all three tasks. Llama ladder: QA only; story cliff does not move from 1B to 8B |\n| Curve shape differs across families at the same scale | **Supported** | Llama differs from the other three families by 4–8× the seed noise floor; Qwen and Mistral are close to each other |\n\n**Practical implication.** Below T ≈ 0.8 temperature is safe on nearly every model tested, but it is a weak diversity\ncontrol. Above T ≈ 1.0, check the specific model: identical settings can give intact output on one model and word\nsalad on another of the same size.\n\nNo API key was available, so all subject models are open-weight checkpoints run locally. GPT, Claude and Gemini were\nnot tested and these results should not be assumed to apply to them.\n\n## 2. Research Question & Hypothesis\n\nTestable sub-hypotheses (H1–H5) are defined in `planning.md`:\n\n- **H1 open-ended:** diversity gain is much smaller above T = 1.2, while coherence at T ≥ 1.4 is 20–40% below peak.\n- **H2 factual QA:** accuracy above 0.7 falls, with little gain in coverage.\n- **H3 code:** useful range ≈ 0.2–0.6, sharp collapse above 0.8.\n- **H4 size:** the upper end of the useful range grows with model size.\n- **H5 family:** curves differ across families at matched size.\n\n## 3. Literature Context\n\nThe resource-finding stage produced no `literature_review.md`, `papers/` or `code/`. The works below are cited **from\nmemory and were not re-checked against the papers**, so treat their details as unverified:\n\n- Holtzman et al. (2020): degeneration at high randomness motivated nucleus sampling.\n- Chen et al. (2021, Codex): best pass@1 at low T, best pass@k at moderate T. Our pass@4 pattern is consistent with this.\n- Renze & Guven (2024): accuracy broadly flat over T in [0, 1].\n- Peeperkorn et al. (2024): temperature only weakly raises story novelty and costs coherence.\n\nThe intended contribution is one controlled grid across tasks, sizes and families, extending to T = 2.0.\n\n## 4. Methodology\n\n### Models\nTen models, plus two controls. All use pure temperature sampling (`top_p=1`, `top_k` off, `min_p=0`,\n`repetition_penalty=1`, `generation_config=\"vllm\"`), with vLLM 0.32 on NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPUs.\n\n| Key | Checkpoint | Params | Precision |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| qwen2.5-0.5b / -1.5b | Qwen/Qwen2.5-{0.5,1.5}B-Instruct | 0.5B / 1.5B | bf16 |\n| qwen2.5-3b / -7b / -14b | Qwen/Qwen2.5-{3,7,14}B-Instruct-AWQ | 3.1B / 7.6B / 14.8B | AWQ-INT4 |\n| llama3.2-1b | unsloth/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct | 1.2B | bf16 |\n| llama3.2-3b | AMead10/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-AWQ | 3.2B | AWQ-INT4 |\n| llama3.1-8b | hugging-quants/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-AWQ-INT4 | 8.0B | AWQ-INT4 |\n| mistral-7b | solidrust/Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3-AWQ | 7.2B | AWQ-INT4 |\n| gemma2-9b | hugging-quants/gemma-2-9b-it-AWQ-INT4 | 9.2B | AWQ-INT4 |\n| *control:* qwen2.5-0.5b-seed2 | same as 0.5B, different sampling seeds | 0.5B | bf16 |\n| *control:* qwen2.5-7b-bf16 | Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct | 7.6B | bf16 |\n\n### Design\n- **Temperatures:** 0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 1.0, 1.2, 1.4, 1.6, 2.0.\n- **Samples:** 4 per (prompt, T); 1 at T = 0, which is deterministic. That is 23,124 samples per run and about 277,000\n  in total across 12 runs. All 12 runs completed all 6,204 (prompt, T) cells.\n- **Stories:** 100 WritingPrompts test prompts, up to 256 tokens.\n- **Factual QA:** 300 closed-book TriviaQA validation questions, up to 24 tokens.\n- **Code:** all 164 HumanEval problems, up to 448 tokens, executed against the official tests in a resource-limited\n  subprocess. Full prompts are in `src/prep_data.py`.\n\n### Metrics\n- **Stories, diversity:** mean pairwise cosine distance of `all-mpnet-base-v2` embeddings (`sem_div`), and distinct-2\n  over pooled samples.\n- **Stories, quality:** Qwen3-8B judge, 1–9, probability-weighted digit, on 2 of 4 samples (24,200 judgments across\n  the 12 runs at 2,100 per run... see note); \"coherent rate\" = share scoring ≥5; lexical validity.\n- **QA:** per-sample accuracy (normalized alias match), coverage@4 (any of 4 correct), distinct answers.\n- **Code:** pass@1, pass@4, syntax validity, distinct programs by AST.\n- **Useful range:** temperatures with quality ≥ 90% of the model's peak, linearly interpolated; T_u is the upper\n  bound. Story quality here is the coherent rate.\n\nAll 25,200 judgments (2,100 per run) passed the logprob validity checks, with none falling back to text parsing.\n\n### Statistics\nItem-level bootstrap (2,000 resamples) for CIs, paired sign-flip permutation tests with Holm correction, and Spearman\ncorrelation for size trends. Curve-shape gaps are compared with two measured floors: a seed replicate and a\nbf16-vs-AWQ control.\n\n### Changes from the plan\n- **14B and 3B models moved to AWQ.** Shared-GPU memory ruled out 14B bf16, and 3B moved to fit a small idle slice.\n  Precision therefore co-varies with size in both ladders (bf16 ≤1.5B, AWQ ≥3B). Section 5.6 shows the effect on curve\n  shape is small but the effect on level is not zero.\n- **Story useful range uses the coherent rate, not the raw judge score.** This was decided after seeing Qwen2.5-0.5B\n  and Llama-1B results, because the judge rarely scores below 3.\n- **The judge rubric was edited once** after inspecting five samples, before any scored judging run.\n- **A seed replicate was added** as a noise floor, and Holm correction and ladder contrasts were added to the analysis.\n  Several analysis choices were therefore made after seeing partial data.\n\n## 5. Results\n\n![All curves](figures/curves_all.png)\n\n### 5.1 H1: open-ended generation\n![Story frontier](figures/story_frontier.png)\n\n| Model | Peak `sem_div` at T | Slope ratio, `sem_div` (1.2–2.0 / 0.2–1.2) | Slope ratio, distinct-2 | Judge drop at 1.2 | at 1.4 | at 2.0 | Coherent rate at 1.2 | at 1.4 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| qwen2.5-0.5b | 1.2 | −1.00 | 0.03 | 44% | 50% | 50% | 0.03 | 0.01 |\n| qwen2.5-1.5b | 1.2 | −0.38 | 0.07 | 53% | 60% | 61% | 0.07 | 0.00 |\n| qwen2.5-3b | 1.4 | −0.45 | 0.23 | 17% | 48% | 62% | 0.86 | 0.22 |\n| qwen2.5-7b | 1.4 | −0.47 | 0.38 | 17% | 53% | 64% | 0.88 | 0.20 |\n| qwen2.5-14b | 1.6 | **+0.98** | 0.43 | 3% | 19% | 63% | 1.00 | 0.82 |\n| llama3.2-1b | 1.0 | −2.09 | 0.05 | 58% | 61% | 62% | 0.04 | 0.00 |\n| llama3.2-3b | 1.0 | −1.41 | 0.12 | 55% | 62% | 62% | 0.10 | 0.00 |\n| llama3.1-8b | 1.0 | −1.65 | 0.09 | 57% | 63% | 63% | 0.07 | 0.00 |\n| mistral-7b | 1.4 | −0.81 | 0.42 | 15% | 49% | 63% | 1.00 | 0.13 |\n| gemma2-9b | 1.4 | −1.02 | 0.26 | 10% | 53% | 64% | 0.95 | 0.09 |\n\n- **Plateau:** supported in the sense that diversity stops improving, but \"plateau\" is the wrong shape. Semantic\n  diversity rises, peaks and falls in 9/10 models. Qwen2.5-14B is the exception, with no slowdown between the two\n  ranges (ratio 0.98, 95% CI 0.69–1.30).\n- **Most of the \"plateau\" is measurement.** Distinct-2 flattens because it reaches its ceiling of 1.0. The fall in\n  `sem_div` arises because word salad from different prompts embeds similarly, so it does not mean the outputs are\n  less varied.\n- **Diversity among coherent outputs keeps rising.** Restricted to prompts where both judged samples are coherent,\n  `sem_div` increases at every T until too few coherent groups remain\n  (`results/analysis/sem_div_coherent_groups.csv`). This is a selected subset, but it suggests coherence loss, not\n  saturation, is what ends useful diversity.\n- **Coherence:** the 20–40% prediction is met only in passing. At T = 1.4 one model falls in or near that range\n  (14B, 19%); nine lose 48–63%. Because the judge's floor is about 3, these figures understate the damage, and the\n  raw scale cannot fall more than about 64%. The coherent rate shows it better: it goes from about 1.0 to about 0 within\n  one or two grid steps.\n\n### 5.2 H2: factual QA\n\n| Model | Greedy acc | Mean T ≤ 0.7 | Mean T 0.8–1.0 | Difference | Holm p | Mean T ≥ 1.2 | Coverage@4, T = 0.7 | Coverage@4, T = 1.0 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| qwen2.5-0.5b | .140 | .134 | .090 | −4.4 pt | <.001 | .030 | .217 | .167 |\n| qwen2.5-1.5b | .370 | .360 | .330 | −2.9 pt | .001 | .198 | .467 | .430 |\n| qwen2.5-3b | .410 | .393 | .367 | −2.6 pt | .001 | .234 | .447 | .463 |\n| qwen2.5-7b | .553 | .545 | .522 | −2.2 pt | .001 | .348 | .593 | .603 |\n| qwen2.5-14b | .627 | .621 | .615 | −0.7 pt | .217 | .512 | .653 | .660 |\n| llama3.2-1b | .317 | .314 | .262 | −5.2 pt | <.001 | .104 | .410 | .383 |\n| llama3.2-3b | .553 | .545 | .497 | −4.8 pt | <.001 | .246 | .613 | .623 |\n| llama3.1-8b | .700 | .692 | .642 | −5.0 pt | <.001 | .346 | .763 | .760 |\n| mistral-7b | .723 | .713 | .693 | −2.0 pt | <.001 | .548 | .753 | .757 |\n| gemma2-9b | .753 | .744 | .719 | −2.5 pt | .001 | .435 | .810 | .800 |\n\nThe p-values above are from 10,000 sign-flip permutations, so \"<.001\" is the resolution limit.\n\nAccuracy falls significantly in 9/10 models, but the loss is small. The degradation is gradual up to about T = 1.2 and\nsteep only beyond it. Sampling does help somewhat at or below 0.7: coverage@4 is 2.7–9.7 points above greedy\naccuracy at T = 0.7. Moving from 0.7 to 1.0 adds nothing on average (changes range from −5.0 to +1.6 points), even\nthough distinct answers keep rising. The extra diversity above 0.7 is mostly more wrong answers.\n\n### 5.3 H3: code generation\n\n| Model | Greedy pass@1 | Best T | T_u (95% CI) | T50 | pass@1 at 0.6 | 0.8 | 1.0 | 1.2 | Holm p, 0.6 vs 1.0 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| qwen2.5-0.5b | .360 | 0.4 | 0.58 (0.41–0.64) | 1.09 | .323 | .273 | .232 | .123 | <.001 |\n| qwen2.5-1.5b | .585 | 0 | 0.81 (0.34–0.87) | 1.29 | .535 | .535 | .415 | .354 | <.001 |\n| qwen2.5-3b | .640 | 0.2 | 1.07 (0.81–1.22) | 1.70 | .630 | .642 | .601 | .570 | .916 |\n| qwen2.5-7b | .768 | 0.7 | 1.42 (1.07–1.46) | 1.79 | .770 | .759 | .739 | .710 | .392 |\n| qwen2.5-14b | .841 | 0 | 1.15 (0.56–1.43) | 1.82 | .787 | .793 | .780 | .750 | .946 |\n| llama3.2-1b | .341 | 0 | 0.44 (0.16–0.68) | 1.17 | .276 | .284 | .230 | .160 | .038 |\n| llama3.2-3b | .549 | 0 | 0.72 (0.31–0.88) | 1.23 | .497 | .486 | .398 | .312 | <.001 |\n| llama3.1-8b | .628 | 0 | 0.69 (0.37–0.91) | 1.26 | .582 | .537 | .532 | .381 | .085 |\n| mistral-7b | .268 | 0.7 | 0.78 (0.14–1.05) | 1.39 | .264 | .238 | .241 | .191 | .946 |\n| gemma2-9b | .610 | 0.4 | 1.34 (0.60–1.41) | 1.65 | .591 | .616 | .607 | .595 | .946 |\n\n- **No sharp collapse just above 0.8.** No model has a significant drop from 0.6 to 0.8 after Holm correction.\n  From 0.6 to 1.0, four models drop significantly, and all are ≤3B.\n- **The lower bound of 0.2 is not supported.** T_lo = 0 for every model. Temperature gives no reliable pass@1 benefit,\n  and five models peak at greedy. T_u confidence intervals are wide because the curves are flat near the threshold.\n- **Pass@4 is where temperature pays off.** At T = 0.8 pass@4 exceeds greedy by up to 16.5 points (Qwen2.5-1.5B),\n  and by 6–15 points for the other models. If several attempts can be tested, moderate T helps.\n- **The real collapse is later and is syntactic.** Pooled across models, syntax errors go from 2% at T = 0.8 to 17% at\n  1.2, 45% at 1.4 and 93% at 2.0.\n\n### 5.4 H4: model size\n![Useful ranges](figures/useful_ranges.png)\n\n| Task | Qwen 0.5B → 14B ΔT_u (95% CI) | Llama 1B → 8B ΔT_u (95% CI) | Spearman ρ, all 10 models (p) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Stories | +0.47 (0.43, 0.55) | −0.05 (−0.09, 0.00) | 0.61 (.060) |\n| QA | +0.87 (0.76, 1.11) | +0.27 (0.04, 0.48) | 0.90 (<.001) |\n| Code | +0.57 (0.00, 0.93) | +0.25 (−0.15, 0.59) | 0.64 (.048) |\n\nLarger models tolerate higher temperature in the Qwen family on all three tasks, though code only just clears zero.\nIn the Llama family it holds for QA only. Story T_u is 1.01 at 1B, 1.00 at 3B and 0.96 at 8B. Spearman values within a\nladder are based on 3–5 points and are not meaningful alone, which is why the bootstrap contrasts are shown.\n\nCode T50, which is less sensitive than T_u, also rises with size: 1.09 → 1.82 for Qwen and 1.17 → 1.26 for Llama.\n\n### 5.5 H5: family differences at matched scale\n![Family comparison](figures/family_matched_diversity.png)\n\nShape gap = mean absolute gap between curves normalized to their own maxima. Floors for `sem_div`: seed 0.031,\nprecision 0.026.\n\n| Pair (7–9B, all AWQ) | `sem_div` shape gap | Ts with CI excluding 0 (of 11) | Peak T |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Qwen 7B vs Llama 8B | 0.220 | 9 | 1.4 vs 1.0 |\n| Llama 8B vs Mistral 7B | 0.205 | 10 | 1.0 vs 1.4 |\n| Llama 8B vs Gemma 9B | 0.118 | 8 | 1.0 vs 1.4 |\n| Qwen 7B vs Gemma 9B | 0.104 | 8 | 1.4 vs 1.4 |\n| Mistral 7B vs Gemma 9B | 0.098 | 9 | 1.4 vs 1.4 |\n| Qwen 7B vs Mistral 7B | 0.046 | 2 | 1.4 vs 1.4 |\n\nThe 1–1.5B and 3B Qwen-vs-Llama pairs show the same result (gaps 0.225 and 0.224). In stories, family matters more\nthan size in this sample: Llama-8B breaks at T_u = 0.96, close to Llama-1B at 1.01, while the other 7–9B models hold\nto 1.17–1.22. The study cannot explain why. Logit scale and vocabulary are candidates, but neither was measured.\n\nEach family is represented by one checkpoint per size, so \"family\" here cannot be separated from individual\ncheckpoint or quantizer differences.\n\n### 5.6 Controls\n- **Seed:** two sampling-seed runs of Qwen2.5-0.5B differ at no temperature for any metric.\n- **Precision:** comparing Qwen2.5-7B bf16 with AWQ, curve shapes are at the seed floor (`sem_div` gap 0.026). Levels\n  do differ: greedy pass@1 is .829 vs .768, and greedy QA .573 vs .553. AWQ therefore does not explain the shape\n  differences, but absolute levels should not be compared across precisions.\n\n### 5.7 Failure modes (pooled over the 10 main models)\n\n| T | Code passes | Syntax errors | Truncated code | QA correct | QA rambling >8 words | Stories truncated | Story non-ASCII chars |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 0.7 | 52% | 2% | 2% | 49% | 3% | 10% | 0.1% |\n| 1.0 | 48% | 4% | 2% | 47% | 2% | 14% | 0.5% |\n| 1.2 | 42% | 17% | 9% | 43% | 4% | 47% | 1.7% |\n| 1.4 | 32% | 45% | 35% | 37% | 12% | 70% | 5.5% |\n| 1.6 | 21% | 68% | 61% | 29% | 28% | 87% | 9.0% |\n| 2.0 | 5% | 93% | 89% | 11% | 58% | 92% | 11.4% |\n\nAbove the cliff, models stop terminating, drift into other languages and emit code tokens in prose. A random high-T\ntoken that breaks the text seems to make further random tokens more likely, which would fit the abrupt shape, though\nthis mechanism was not tested directly.\n\n## 6. Limitations\n\n- **One judge, compressed scale.** The Qwen3-8B judge rarely scores below 3, and it shares a lineage with the Qwen\n  subjects. A manual audit of 16 boundary stories agreed with the ≥5 threshold on 12 (75%), with the judge slightly\n  conservative. That audit was by the researcher-agent, on one model, and is small (`results/judge_audit.md`).\n- **The embedding metric is misleading on degenerate text,** as described in 5.1.\n- **Family and checkpoint are confounded,** and precision co-varies with size.\n- **Mistral's low code score** (.268) is below what its QA score would suggest. Restoring prompt imports raised it\n  only to .280, and failures are mostly real assertion errors, but the cause is unexplained. It may be specific to\n  this checkpoint or prompt, so its code level should not be taken as representative of Mistral-7B.\n- **Scope.** Pure temperature only, with no top-p or top-k. Tests cover short stories, one prompt per task, and 4\n  samples per cell. T = 0 uses one sample, and a single seed replicate is a thin noise floor. vLLM batch composition can\n  also change outputs slightly.\n- **Grid resolution.** Steps of 0.2 above T = 0.8 limit how precisely a cliff can be placed, and T_u depends on the\n  90% threshold choice. Bootstrap CIs on story T_u are narrow partly because of this interpolation.\n- **Multiple comparisons** were corrected within tests but not across the full set of hypotheses.\n- **Contamination:** TriviaQA, HumanEval and WritingPrompts are public and may be in training data. This matters\n  little for curve shapes, more for absolute levels.\n\n## 7. Conclusions & Next Steps\n\nTemperature's useful range is set by where each model breaks, and that varies widely. There is no universal threshold,\nand the common 0.7 default is safe rather than optimal. Single-attempt code and QA do best at low T, while pass@4 and\ncoverage@4 benefit from moderate T.\n\nSuggested follow-ups:\n\n1. Measure logit scale and the top-token margin per model to test whether they predict the cliff location.\n2. Add more checkpoints per family to separate family from checkpoint.\n3. Run hosted frontier models through the same grid when an API key is available.\n4. Replace `sem_div` with a measure that does not collapse on degenerate text, and add human coherence ratings.\n5. Sample T at 0.05 steps around each model's cliff to test how abrupt it really is.\n\n## 8. Reproducibility\n\n- **Environment:** Python 3.12, vLLM 0.32.0, torch 2.13.0, transformers 5.19.0 (`results/environment.txt`, `uv.lock`).\n  Seeds are set in `src/worker.py` and `src/analyze.py`.\n- **Hardware:** RTX A6000 (48 GB), shared; ~3 h 15 min wall time. Runs used 4–22 GB slices and several parallel\n  workers, so timing does not generalize. No API cost.\n- **Run:** `./run_all.sh`. It needs an OpenAI-compatible Qwen3-8B server on port 8011.\n- **Outputs:** raw generations in `results/gens/`, scores in `results/scores/`, judgments in `results/judge/`, tables\n  in `results/analysis/`.\n\n## References\n\nCited from memory; not verified in this session.\n\n- Chen, M. et al. (2021). Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code.\n- Holtzman, A. et al. (2020). The Curious Case of Neural Text Degeneration. ICLR.\n- Peeperkorn, M. et al. (2024). Is Temperature the Creativity Parameter of Large Language Models?\n- Renze, M. & Guven, E. (2024). The Effect of Sampling Temperature on Problem Solving in Large Language Models.\n\nDatasets: `euclaise/writingprompts`, `mandarjoshi/trivia_qa`, `openai/openai_humaneval`.\nEOF\npython3 - <<'EOF'\np='REPORT.md'; s=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"on 2 of 4 samples (24,200 judgments across\\n  the 12 runs at 2,100 per run... see note); \\\"coherent rate\\\"\",\"on 2 of 4 samples; \\\"coherent rate\\\"\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"see note\" REPORT.md; .venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport os; os.environ.setdefault('NUMPY_MADVISE_HUGEPAGE','0')\nimport pandas as pd\nc=pd.read_csv('/tmp/h3_run1.csv').set_index('model')\nbase=pd.read_csv('results/analysis/curves.csv') if False else None\nh=pd.read_csv('/tmp/curves_run1.csv')\np=h[(h.task=='code')&(h.metric=='passk')].pivot_table(index='model',columns='T',values='mean')\nprint((p[0.8]-p[0.0]).round(3).to_string())\nq=h[(h.task=='qa')&(h.metric=='cover')].pivot_table(index='model',columns='T',values='mean'); print((q[1.0]-q[0.7]).round(3).to_string())\nprint(c.sort_values('p_0.6_vs_0.8_holm')[['p_0.6_vs_0.8_holm','p_0.6_vs_1.0_holm']].round(3).to_string())\nEOF","description":"Write REPORT.md and check quoted figures","timeout":60000}}}
